WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.350
Avoiding spoilers for a major event film like
Star Wars Episode IX: The Rise of Skywalker

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:06.720
can be a little difficult.

00:00:07.069 --> 00:00:10.680
For those involved with the production of
Star Wars toys, going into the theater cold

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.740
can be next to impossible, but that doesn't
mean they don't try.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.100
The good folks at CinemaBlend recently spoke
to a couple individuals in the business of

00:00:18.109 --> 00:00:22.039
Star Wars merchandising, and they were quick
to confirm that ducking those spoilers can

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:23.020
be a real hassle.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.640
Designing toys is a very lengthy process.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.960
The long lead times mean that designers will
be introduced to new characters long before

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.220
the general public, sometimes years before.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.600
This can often mean that it's necessary for
plot points to be given away in order for

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.200
a toy's manufacturer to understand why it
must be designed in a certain way.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:44.660
However, according to Lucasfilm Senior VP
for Licensing Paul Southern, that doesn't

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:47.280
mean that toy makers are forced to hear spoilers.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.310
At a recent event revealing a portion of Rise
of Skywalker's toy line, Southern indicated

00:00:52.310 --> 00:00:55.890
that there's a surprising amount of leeway
given to those who are extra-determined to

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:56.920
avoid spoilers.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:57.960
He told CinemaBlend:

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.700
"You need to have a basic understanding of
the architecture [of each toy], but there

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.840
are elements that are 'No, I don't really
want to know about that.'

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:08.859
There are actually people who are working
on the film products that want to know nothing

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.760
and they actually will not go to meetings."

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.560
It seems like that would make it impossible
to do your job without going to meetings,

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:15.880
but apparently, it's not.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:16.900
It's just difficult.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:17.980
Southern said,

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:21.920
"We have some briefings, we get some information
that allows us to make the right decisions,

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:25.679
and also allows us to see the role of the
character [so] that we can reflect that in

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.580
the toys and the rest of the merchandise.

00:01:27.909 --> 00:01:30.700
But there are people on my team that [say],
'I don't want to be there.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.539
I'll work on it, and I'll develop it but I
just don't want to know the context because

00:01:34.539 --> 00:01:36.799
I don't want it to be spoiled when I go and
see the film.'

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.040
It's a fine balance."

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:42.200
You might think it would be tough for a head
honcho like Southern himself to avoid facing

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.740
the full brunt of every spoiler, but apparently,
he finds ways to dodge the big reveals too.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:47.400
He said,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.880
"There are elements that I don't want to know
about, because ultimately I want to have a

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:52.740
great experience when I go to the cinema.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.260
But I know enough about the architecture of
the film, so that we know what to be focusing

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:56.760
on."

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:01.040
Of course, it's not like Lucasfilm wants to
dole out spoilers left and right; the fewer

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:04.259
of them get out there, the smaller the chance
that they'll make their way to the general

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:04.760
public.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.810
With that in mind, it's interesting to know
that the development process for Star Wars

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:13.150
merchandise can take a little longer than
it does for non-tentpole-related toys, because

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:16.820
the studio will often be intentionally vague
when meeting with the designers.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:21.459
Take it from Mike Ballog, Senior Director
of Global Marketing for Hasbro, which is handling

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:23.270
the Rise of Skywalker toy line.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.319
He says that design elements of certain toys
will often have to be changed midway through

00:02:27.319 --> 00:02:31.730
the development process for that very reason,
a dynamic that has just kind of been factored

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:32.800
into the way they work.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:33.800
According to him:

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:38.140
"Sometimes, we'll go down the direction that
we think is going to be the best expression,

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:41.300
and then we'll put a model in front of the
[studio] guys and we'll have a conversation,

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:44.280
they'll be like 'well, now he's going to do
this.'

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:46.659
We're like 'oh,' and we'll go back and fix
it.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:48.080
It's an iterative process."

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:52.360
So the next time you're at the store looking
at Star Wars toys, take a moment to appreciate

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:56.909
all the hard work that's gone into the creations,
and the even harder work that those designers

00:02:56.909 --> 00:02:59.560
put in to avoid spoiling themselves for the
new movie.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:04.220
Star Wars Episode IX: The Rise of Skywalker
hits the big screen on December 20.

